{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012A4YUHFwn2tFtKFGgYowfe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good, the directory exists. Now let me write a comprehensive LaTeX paper based on the experiment data and paper framing. I need to cover all experiments and create appropriate tables for each.\n\nLet me organize the experiments:\n\n1. **SciERC violation rate (minimal prompt)** - Table T1: Per-relation DR violation rates\n2. **SciERC violation rate (schema-informed prompt)** - Table T2: Schema-informed prompt effect\n3. **DocRED violation rate (minimal prompt)** - Table T3a: DocRED violation landscape\n\n\n4. **DocRED violation rate (schema-informed prompt)** - Integrated into the analysis\n5. **SciERC unconstrained baseline** - Table T4: 4-way ablation with grammar-only and semantic-only variants\n6. **SciERC full S1** - Table T4_full and T5 for CF subtype analysis\n7. **SciERC full S2** - Table T6 comparing S1 versus S2\n8. **DocRED unconstrained baseline** - Table T7: DocRED ablation with grammar-only variants\n9. **DocRED full S1** - Table T7_full and T8 for fallback comparison\n10. **Llama SciERC unconstrained** - Table T9: Cross-model results with full S1 variant\n11. **Llama DocRED unconstrained** - Table T10: 2x2 matrix with full S1 and dual-factor analysis in T11\n12. **S2 pilot** - Excluded from the paper\n13. **Post-hoc filtering baseline** - Table T12: Multi-dimensional comparison\n14. **Bootstrap CI** - Table T13 with confidence intervals\n15. **DocRED schema compilation R2** - Removed and not included\n\nNow I'm ready to write the paper with this structure. Looking at the ablation study results for SciERC, I'm tracking how different constraint types affect the error metrics. The unconstrained baseline shows entity and relation errors, but when I apply grammar-only constraints, those errors drop to zero while maintaining similar F1 scores. The semantic-only approach reduces detection errors significantly but introduces constraint violations. The full schema constraints in both variants nearly eliminate detection errors while keeping F1 performance stable or slightly improved.\n\nNow examining the DocRED dataset, I'm seeing a similar pattern where grammar-only constraints eliminate entity and relation errors, though the semantic-only approach shows higher violation rates. The full schema constraint in S1 achieves the best balance, reducing detection errors to around 10% while maintaining strong F1 scores.\n\nLooking at the cross-model results with Llama, the SciERC dataset shows dramatic improvements with the full constraint—detection errors drop by over 99%—while DocRED results remain more stable across both unconstrained and constrained settings.\n\nNow examining the post-hoc analysis and bootstrap significance tests. The DocRED relation F1 improves substantially with statistical significance, gaining 0.50 percentage points with high confidence, while SciERC shows minimal changes that don't reach significance. I'm preparing to write this up carefully, sticking to the EMNLP format and pulling all numbers directly from the experiment data.\n\nWriting the LaTeX paper...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8229,"cache_read_input_tokens":40517,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8229},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00717ec3-027e-4183-9490-a5e4aa0150c4","uuid":"fc5125ee-cd2c-4dca-9d2f-23ea9ca63b08"}
